<%!--
  The second of a link's locks: see `Appender.link/4`. One statement over every event id, at
  any size, because the inserts run in batches of 1,000 and a lock per batch would lose the
  order across them. `event_id` order is the one `lock_deleted_events` takes these rows in, so
  a link and a hard delete cannot cycle on them. KEY SHARE, as the foreign key check would
  take, so links do not wait on each other, and FOR UPDATE on the delete's side conflicts
  with it. A row a hard delete has removed is skipped, which the caller's count answers. Any
  row lock needs UPDATE on the table, so a link needs it on `events`.
--%>
SELECT event_id
FROM "<%= schema %>".events
WHERE event_id = ANY($1::uuid[])
ORDER BY event_id
FOR KEY SHARE;
